architectures, a feature of computer architectures
for over two decades, I think over three decades.

Why yes, three decades is even too small:

:- 1959-1961: Rice Computer

:- Rice Institute Computer (R1) was the first machine
:- with a segmented memory architecture. It was a
:- one-off design; 54 bits x 8K words of
:- electrostatic memory, later replaced with 32 KW of
:- magnetic core memory.

Odds of that being before you were born seem
splendid, on slender evidence.

A pointer in a segmented address architecture, is a
structure, not a scalar, so to say that it is "zero"
isn't even meaningful.

Such minor players in the computer architecture game
as, say, Intel, have employed segmented
architectures, yet they have also had C compilers
implemented on those architectures.

